lesson g6-inverse-proportion v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=4 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Inverse proportion: more pipes, less time"
  goal "Recognise when one amount goes down as another goes up with their product staying the same, and use that product to find a missing value."

  look {
    scene 27x13 {
      waterwheel wheel paddles=8 lit=0 at=canvas(1, 1)
      table data cols=2 head=["Pipes", "Hours"] cells=["1", "12", "2", "6", "3", "4", "4", "3"] right-of=wheel gap=2
    }
    say """
      The mill pond below the water wheel is filled by pipes of the same size. One pipe takes 12
      hours, two take 6, three take 4. When the pipes double the hours halve, and pipes times hours
      is always 12. That is inverse proportion: the product stays the same. In direct proportion it
      is the quotient that stays the same.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice inverse.pipes-mill-pond count=1 seed=3
    level easy {
      worked inverse.bridge-menders w=3 d=8 w2=4 level=medium
    }
    practice inverse.bridge-menders count=1 seed=4
    practice inverse.bucket-table count=1 seed=5
    level hard {
      show inverse.bridge-menders w=6 d=6 w2=3
    }
    practice inverse.boat-slower-trip count=1 seed=6
    show inverse.direct-or-inverse kind=2 a=3 k=12 easy-kind=0 easy-a=2 hard-kind=1 hard-a=2 hard-k=24
    practice inverse.pipes-graph count=1 seed=7
    show inverse.bridge-menders w=6 d=4 w2=8 easy-w=3 easy-d=4 easy-w2=6 hard-w=6 hard-d=8 hard-w2=12
    practice inverse.boat-slower-trip count=1 seed=9
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show inverse.double-pipes-mistake p=2 h=8 k=2 easy-h=6 hard-p=3 hard-h=12 hard-k=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show inverse.menders-join-later w=6 d=10 a=4 j=3 easy-w=4 easy-d=12 easy-a=2 easy-j=6 hard-d=12 hard-a=4 hard-j=2
  }
  remember {
    say "In inverse proportion one amount times the other stays the same, so twice as many take half as long."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Inverse proportion is the first relationship a child meets where more gives less. The common
    slip is to treat it as direct: twice the pipes, twice the time. Ask before any working whether
    the answer should be bigger or smaller, and then find the product (pipes times hours, workers
    times days, the length of a trip) and share it. In the table question the step between columns
    is not the same, which rules out adding. In the two-star question the reason is that more pipes
    pour faster. In the three-star question the work is counted in worker-days: some are done before
    the others join, and the rest is shared by everyone. If the product still feels strange, stay
    here another day with pipes and hours only. Off the page, share a job such as folding washing
    between one, two and three people and time it.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level two or three pipes share 12 or 24 hours, a bridge mended by 3 workers in 8 days is worked in the teacher's pen, more workers join rather than leave, the buckets are 2, 3 and 4 litres, the boat goes slower, the table is direct, the graph asks for 12 pipes, Ana doubles 3 pipes, and 6 workers join 4 after 2 days of a 12-day job."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level four or six pipes share 24 or 36 hours, the bridge is asked three times, apart, and workers may leave, the buckets are 2, 6 and 12 litres, the boat goes faster, the table is inverse, the graph starts at 24 hours, Ana trebles 3 pipes, and 2 workers join 6 after 4 days of a 12-day job."
  }
}
